---
/**
 * REWRITTEN TO SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md, 26 Nov 2026 (a founder: rewrite the
 * remaining area pages to the writing Kaspar comments on; ops rulings D3, no
 * "carpentry", and D7, no sources in a sentence and no council or history
 * narration). The opening is one paragraph of two sentences. Every date,
 * builder, listing grade, count and appraisal quote is gone (The Grove's 1680s,
 * Pevsner and Cherry, Holly Terrace's dates, Highpoint, Grove Lodge's lease
 * line). Five sections, each headed fitting, room, place: loft rooms, alcoves,
 * narrow rooms above shops, listed homes, parking and access. The listed and
 * parking sections send the reader to Camden, Haringey or Islington by street.
 * FAQ questions kept; answers rewritten to open on the answer. Prices and
 * their section untouched. Drawings: mansard-section (loft rooms) and
 * high-street-shops (above shops) kept; holly-terrace moved to the access
 * section, whose words now carry the steps; the alcove section is plain text,
 * as no Highgate alcove drawing exists and another page's would repeat it.
 * FACT LIST (claim: source).
 *   - Highgate Village and the Archway Road as two parts of Highgate: facts
 *     file, "Which council covers which part".
 *   - Many Southwood Terrace and Bisham Gardens houses have a loft room with a
 *     sloping ceiling: facts file Group 1 items 6 and 8, Group 2 item 5.
 *   - Hanging where the ceiling is highest, low drawers or cupboards under the
 *     slope, pull-out drawers: his facts table, loft rows (23 Nov Camden,
 *     26 Nov Islington).
 *   - Doors planned to open fully under the slope: his facts table, drawers
 *     behind hinged doors row; his wardrobe guide line "Check how every door
 *     opens beneath the slope and what can be reached through it".
 *   - Georgian houses on Highgate West Hill and the High Street, older ones on
 *     The Grove: facts file Group 1 item 4.
 *   - Alcoves differ in width or depth, each unit designed for its own side:
 *     his facts table, alcoves row (26 Nov Islington).
 *   - Floating shelves or framed shelving with sides and back: his alcove
 *     guide (his 14 Oct rewrite), "Floating shelves" and "Framed shelving".
 *   - Flats or narrow townhouses above a shop on the High Street and the
 *     Archway Road: facts file Group 1 items 3 and 6 (H1 2.2, 2.4).
 *   - Shallow cupboards in a narrow room leave space to walk past and open the
 *     doors; a wardrobe can run to the ceiling: his facts table, lower-ground
 *     and period-house rows (26 Nov Islington).
 *   - Which council by street (Camden south of the High Street with The Grove
 *     and Highgate West Hill; Haringey north with North Road and Southwood
 *     Lane; Islington around Hornsey Lane): facts file Group 3 item 1.
 *   - Ask the council about listed building consent, especially for original
 *     skirting or panelling: his facts table, listed homes row (26 Nov).
 *   - A wardrobe can stop below an original cornice, moulding visible: his
 *     facts table, cornice row (10 Nov Hampstead, 23 Nov Camden).
 *   - Before fitting day, unloading, parking and the way in agreed; who
 *     arranges a permit agreed; floors and stairs protected along the delivery
 *     route: his facts table, access rows (Hampstead 8 and 10 Nov, Camden
 *     23 Nov, Islington 26 Nov).
 *   - Furniture in sections, assembled in the room, for steps and narrow
 *     stairs: his facts table, sections row (10 Nov Hampstead). Holly Terrace
 *     reached up steps: facts file Group 1 item 5.
 *   - Council links: Camden listed buildings (C2), Camden parking zones (C5),
 *     Camden trade permits (C3), Haringey planning permission (H6), Haringey
 *     parking zones (H5), Haringey permission to park (H3). All checked 200 on
 *     26 Nov 2026 (Camden's answer 403 to a bare client and 200 to a browser).
 *     Islington is named without a link: no Islington page on this page or in
 *     the facts file fits (I7 is a 2002 design guide).
 *   - FAQ: Shaker definition and frame widths, his built-in wardrobe guide,
 *     word for word; door style and finish the customer's choice, his facts
 *     table (23 Nov Camden); narrow alcoves take fewer doors and divisions,
 *     shelf openings 250 to 350 mm and 350 to 450 mm or more, his alcove guide;
 *     wall colour or contrast, samples in daylight and evening light, his
 *     alcove guide, colour section.
 *   No Ask brackets: every figure on the page is his.
 *
 * Highgate, 17 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review link
 * (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaHighgateCheck until he has read it and his changes are in. Nothing goes
 * live before he has approved every area page (Denis, 15 Nov 2026).
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL from the start (a founder's go, 17 Nov: "next";
 * Denis, 15 Nov: "use the same design and structure for the next articles";
 * ops README, "The structure rule, applied"; ops SS_AREA_PAGE_RULES_v1.0.md,
 * a draft, read for its plan items): address /areas/highgate/ with 301s from
 * /carpenter-highgate/ (netlify.toml and the Worker's list); the title,
 * heading, kicker and description lead with fitted wardrobes, then alcove
 * units (the reasons are in areas.ts); the opening carries "bespoke
 * carpentry" and "bespoke joinery" once each; a line drawing beside each
 * section that describes something (src/data/area-drawings.ts: a row of
 * narrow shops on a high street, a terrace of Georgian houses reached up
 * steps, a mansard room in section with a wardrobe under its slope, id
 * "mansard-section"), none
 * where a section is rules or access only; the guide prices as cards;
 * register photos and one labelled AI image (ssp-037 and ssp-039, and AI
 * arched alcove units, Figma node 36:2).
 *
 * Sources. Every local fact from the ops facts file
 * research/area-pages-2026-11-14/facts-highgate-2026-11-14.md, read 14 Nov
 * 2026:
 *   Camden's Highgate Conservation Area Appraisal and Management Strategy
 *     ("C1"): the borough boundary through the middle of the High Street,
 *     the Camden side's Pond Square, The Grove and Highgate West Hill; The
 *     Grove, Nos 1 to 6 built in the 1680s, "the finest group in Highgate"
 *     (Pevsner and Cherry); Holly Terrace, eleven listed houses of c1806 to
 *     1813 reached up steps from Highgate West Hill; Nos 17 to 21 High
 *     Street, a red brick terrace of 1733 with raised ground floors; Bisham
 *     Gardens, two-storey red brick terraces with projecting square bays,
 *     built with a mansard attic behind the front gable; Highgate Hill at
 *     129.2 m above sea level; Nos 45 and 46 Highgate West Hill (1729), "high
 *     mansard roofs behind parapets"; Bisham Gardens' dormers and rooflights;
 *     the large chimneys of Nos 81 and 82 Highgate West Hill; Grove Lodge,
 *     Nos 7 to 15 The Grove, an 1880s terrace converted to flats.
 *   Haringey's Highgate Conservation Area Character Appraisal, October 2012
 *     ("H1"): the north side of the High Street, North Road, North Hill and
 *     Southwood Lane in Haringey; "the three storey narrow fronted terrace
 *     townhouse, frequently with a shop at street level" as the primary
 *     building type (2.2); both sides "a single, unified historic village
 *     core, divided only by an early political boundary" (6.4); Southwood
 *     Terrace, grade II narrow two-storey townhouses of the early 19th
 *     century, "Most of the houses in the terrace have added mansard roofs"
 *     (6.78); Highpoint I and II, grade I, by Lubetkin and Tecton, 1935 and
 *     1938, Highpoint II with duplex flats of two-storey living rooms (6.128);
 *     the Archway Road's late Victorian and Edwardian shop parades with flats
 *     above (2.4). Islington's Highgate Hill / Hornsey Lane guidelines ("I7").
 *   Haringey's permission to park page: "ideal for landlords or tradespeople",
 *     valid on a chosen street ("H3").
 *   Camden's listed buildings and trade permit pages ("C2", "C3"); Haringey's
 *     planning permission and permission to park pages ("H6", "H3").
 * Left out: Selina S's Highgate review (it names Kas, so it cannot be quoted
 * whole, and the founders ruled out describing a review in our words), the
 * Islington corner (one guideline document only), Holly Lodge (not read),
 * and any Article 4 claim (Haringey's 2012 appraisal had none in force;
 * current position not read).
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS. From what he approved: the alcove units page's lines "No two
 * alcoves in a period house are the same width. Each unit is built to its own
 * measurements and cut to follow the wall."; the wardrobe guide's lines
 * on sloping ceilings ("Check how every door opens beneath the slope and
 * what can be reached through it. A clean arrangement of usable compartments
 * is more valuable than a complicated pattern of small doors following every
 * change in ceiling height."), his Shaker doors paragraph, and the alcove
 * guide's lines on narrow recesses and on colour (the questions); the
 * guarantee; the fitted wardrobe and alcove price lines as approved on
 * Hampstead. One conduct line: parking, deliveries and access agreed before
 * installation, as on Camden, Belsize Park, Kentish Town and Swiss Cottage.
 * Everything else is new copy for him to check.
 *
 * CHECKED 17 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, facts against
 * the facts file and approved text, the swap test against the seven other
 * area pages). Changed on their findings: the new drawing's first id,
 * "mansard-wardrobe", clashed with Primrose Hill's and replaced it there, so
 * it is "mansard-section"; The Grove's 1680s houses are not "Georgian", so
 * that section says period houses; "eleven" is 11; Nos 17, 19 and 21, not
 * "17 to 21"; the Archway Road's late Victorian and Edwardian parades (H1
 * 2.4) back the heading's "Victorian"; the Islington corner is named;
 * Highpoint is not called "the highest grade"; the alcove and colour lines
 * are his words; the mansard and alcove sections carry Highgate-only facts
 * (Nos 45 and 46 Highgate West Hill's high mansards, Bisham Gardens' dormers,
 * Nos 81 and 82's large chimneys); the lease line hangs on Grove Lodge's
 * conversion; the altitude went; borough, appraisal, listed and listed
 * building consent are explained once.
 *
 * Street names are the councils' documents', describing the houses. None is
 * a place we claim to have worked.
 *
 * FAQ sources, all three new, none on another area page: "What are Shaker
 * style wardrobe doors?" (Google People also ask, shaker wardrobe doors),
 * "What can go in a narrow alcove?" and "Should alcoves be lighter or
 * darker?" (People also ask, alcove shelving ideas: "What to put in a narrow
 * alcove?", made a whole question); UK, English, desktop, 17 Nov 2026. The
 * boxes for fitted wardrobes highgate and bespoke wardrobes highgate held
 * only costs and other companies' names. Answers from what he approved: his
 * Shaker doors paragraph (word for word), and the alcove guide's lines on a
 * narrow recess, unequal alcoves and colour.
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/highgate")!;

const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      We design and install <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">fitted wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> and shelving for homes in Highgate Village and along the Archway Road. If you have a sloping loft ceiling, alcoves that don't match or a narrow flat above a shop, we'll design the joinery to fit.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Loft rooms" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes and drawers for loft rooms in Highgate</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="mansard-section">
      <p>
        Many houses on Southwood Terrace and Bisham Gardens have a loft room with a sloping ceiling. We can put the hanging space where the ceiling is highest, with low pull-out drawers under the slope for jumpers and bed linen. We'll also plan the doors so each one opens fully under the slope and you can reach what's behind it.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Alcoves" level={4}>Alcove units for period houses in Highgate Village</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Highgate Village has Georgian houses on Highgate West Hill and the High Street, and older ones on The Grove. If the alcoves beside your chimney breast differ in width or depth, we'll design each unit for its own side. Above the cupboards, you can choose floating shelves or <Link href="/journal/alcove-units-guide/">framed shelving</Link> with its own sides and back.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Above the shops" level={4}>Fitted cupboards for narrow rooms above Highgate's shops</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="high-street-shops">
      <p>
        On the High Street and the Archway Road, many homes sit above a shop, in narrow townhouses or flats. If the bedroom is narrow, we can design shallower cupboards so there's still room to walk past and open the doors. Running them to the ceiling adds space on top for suitcases without taking up more of the floor.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Fitted joinery in listed homes across Highgate</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        If you're planning fitted wardrobes, alcove units or shelving, check with your council whether your home is listed. South of the High Street, including The Grove and Highgate West Hill, see <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" {...EXT}>Camden Council's listed buildings page</Link>. North of it, including North Road and Southwood Lane, see <Link href="https://haringey.gov.uk/planning-building-control/planning/planning-applications/pre-application-guidance/when-you-need-planning-permission" {...EXT}>Haringey Council's planning permission page</Link>; around Hornsey Lane, ask Islington Council.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If your home is listed, ask that council whether the joinery needs listed building consent before we start. That matters most where fitting it would mean removing or altering original skirting or panelling. If there's an original cornice, we can stop a wardrobe below it, leaving the moulding in view.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>Parking, steps and access on fitting days in Highgate</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="holly-terrace">
      <p>
        Before fitting day, we'll agree with you where we unload and park, and how the furniture comes in. You can look up your parking zone on <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>Camden Council's parking zones page</Link> or <Link href="https://haringey.gov.uk/parking/cpzs" {...EXT}>Haringey Council's parking zones page</Link>.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If we need a permit, we'll agree who applies for it: a <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" {...EXT}>Camden trade permit</Link> or a <Link href="https://haringey.gov.uk/parking/parking-permits/daily-temp/permission-park" {...EXT}>Haringey permission to park permit</Link>. On the day, we protect the floors and stairs along the delivery route before anything comes in.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        For homes up a flight of steps, as on Holly Terrace, or up a narrow stair, we can bring the furniture in sections. We then assemble it in the room.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>Starting prices for Highgate wardrobes and alcove units</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10 years. Hardware is on the manufacturer's warranty, and doors and drawers are adjusted in the first 2 years. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> shows what moves the price of a fitted wardrobe.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="What are Shaker style wardrobe doors?">
        Shaker doors have a flat centre panel surrounded by a simple frame. A slimmer frame creates a lighter look; wider framing gives the doors more presence. You choose the finish and handles, and our <Link href="/journal/built-in-wardrobe-ideas/">built-in wardrobe ideas</Link> show Shaker doors with knobs and with bar handles.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What can go in a narrow alcove?">
        A narrow alcove can take open shelves or a cupboard, with fewer doors and divisions than a wide one. For books and smaller objects, we'd start with openings of around 250 to 350 mm between the shelves. Larger display pieces suit 350 to 450 mm or more.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="Should alcoves be lighter or darker?">
        It depends on how much you want the units to stand out. In the wall colour, alcove units blend into the room, and a contrasting colour gives them more emphasis. Look at painted samples in daylight and under your evening lighting before you choose, rather than on a screen.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
